**Study:** Revisit [Engineering Dynamics — MIT 2.003SC, Fall 2011](../sources/Engineering-Dynamics-MIT-2-003SC.lingua#r_37MSK6EJHRV95GXWA23P4ZJPZ3)'s demonstrations, recitation 10 and oscillator identification examples. Use the earlier metrology principles when choosing sensors, calibration and uncertainty accounts.

Define the observable before selecting an instrument: displacement of which point relative to which frame, angular position, velocity, acceleration or force. A sensor's range, bandwidth, sampling, mounting and calibration affect what is actually observed. Numerically differentiating a noisy position twice can magnify measurement error; an accelerometer's output also depends on orientation and gravity.

A sampled sinusoid can be confused with a different frequency when sampling aliases the motion. A frequency above twice the signal bandwidth is a necessary ideal sampling condition, not proof of adequate amplitude/phase measurement in a real filtered and noisy acquisition chain. Give the analog filtering, clock and record duration their own roles.

A natural frequency alone identifies a stiffness-to-mass ratio in a one-mode model, not both parameters separately. Add independent mass or static-deflection evidence. Estimate damping using several observations and test the model at a different amplitude or forcing condition from the one used to fit it.

**Work:** For a low-energy educational oscillator, specify and perform a measurement where suitable equipment is available, retaining raw data and calibration information. Compare frequency, amplitude and phase predictions with uncertainty; inspect residuals and a second plausible model. Distinguish performed observations from proposed work. The following studies develop complementary mathematical and machine-focused questions, then return to a common dynamics review.

